questions.question:
Discuss cerebral palsy under the following headings:
(a) definition;
(b) causes and risk factors;
(c) classification (motor type, topography, severity);
(d) clinical features and associated problems.
(e) Explain why the clinical picture of cerebral palsy changes as the child grows although the brain lesion is non-progressive.
(f) Explain why a child with spastic quadriplegia has feeding and speech difficulties.
(g) Distinguish between spastic and dyskinetic cerebral palsy.
(h) Distinguish between spastic diplegia and spastic hemiplegia.

A. Describe the stomach as the site of gastrin action under the following headings:
A(i) the regions and cells involved;
A(ii) synthesis and processing of gastrin;
A(iii) stimuli and inhibitors of release;
A(iv) functions and mechanism of action.
B. B(i) State how these cells and regions relate to the derangements of gastrin (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, autoimmune gastritis and other causes of raised gastrin).
C. C(i) Explain why gastrin disturbance affects distant structures and mucosal growth.

A. Describe the gastrointestinal tract as an endocrine organ under the following headings:
A(i) definition and distribution of its endocrine cells;
A(ii) the families of gastrointestinal peptides;
A(iii) the principal hormones (gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, GIP): size, source, stimulus and actions.
B. B(i) State how the regions of the gut relate to the hormones made there and to mucosal growth and disease.
C. C(i) Explain how these hormones act on distant structures and the mechanisms by which they signal.

A. Describe the pathway of insulin under the following headings:
A(i) origin and chemistry (precursor, chains and processing steps);
A(ii) release from the beta cell (glucose-stimulated mechanism, other stimulators and inhibitors);
A(iii) intracellular signalling and actions on target tissues;
A(iv) degradation.
B. B(i) State the tests used to assess insulin function.
C. C(i) Explain the biochemical basis of the features of diabetes mellitus when insulin is deficient.

A. Describe peroxisomal β-oxidation under the following headings:
A(i) occurrence and role in animals and in plants;
A(ii) entry and activation of substrates;
A(iii) the three enzymatic reactions.
B(i) Explain how the properties of the peroxisomal enzymes limit the extent and efficiency of oxidation.
C(i) Explain why the products must reach another organelle and how they are transported there.

A. Describe the first enzymatic difficulty in the β-oxidation of linoleyl CoA under the following headings:
A(i) the point at which it arises and the products formed before it;
A(ii) the double bond involved;
A(iii) the enzyme that overcomes it and the conversion.
B(i) State the site in the pathway at which the intermediate is blocked.
C(i) Explain the basis of the energy deficit that follows.

A. Describe the chylomicron under the following headings:
A(i) origin and assembly (site, constituents, apolipoprotein);
A(ii) course from the enterocyte to the blood;
A(iii) maturation and exchange with HDL;
A(iv) fate and distribution (lipoprotein lipase, remnant).
B. B(i) State the sites at which lipoprotein lipase acts.
C. C(i) Explain the basis of the change in chylomicron composition and size as triglyceride is removed and why dietary long-chain fat reaches the liver last.

A. Describe the first oxidation of the β-oxidation cycle under the following headings:
A(i) the enzymes and their specificities;
A(ii) the mechanism;
A(iii) the product.
B(i) Explain how the reduced cofactor is reoxidised and how ATP is obtained.
C(i) Distinguish between the peroxisomal first oxidation and the mitochondrial first oxidation.

A. Give an overview of the sites at which the successive stages of fatty acid oxidation occur under the following headings:
A(i) activation;
A(ii) membrane transfer;
A(iii) the β-oxidation cycle;
A(iv) plants.
B(i) Relate the sites to the chain lengths handled by the enzymes at each site.
C(i) Explain why the stage of membrane transfer is central to the later mitochondrial stages.

A. Describe the route taken by acyl groups shortened by peroxisomal β-oxidation to reach the mitochondrion under the following headings:
A(i) the substrates and their entry into the peroxisome;
A(ii) the enzymes that modify the acyl groups;
A(iii) the course out of the peroxisome;
A(iv) the destination.
B(i) State the enzyme whose specificity determines where the peroxisomal process stops.
C(i) Explain the basis of the reduced energy yield of peroxisomal oxidation.

A. Describe the adrenal (suprarenal) glands under the following headings:
A(i) location and subdivisions;
A(ii) hormones secreted by each part and their functions;
A(iii) control of secretion.
B. B(i) State how the parts and hormones relate to the common pathologies (primary hyperaldosteronism, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease, androgen excess).
C. C(i) Explain why disease of a distant gland or therapy can produce adrenal hormone excess and functional deficit.

A. Describe the eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, leukotrienes) under the following headings:
A(i) origin and precursors;
A(ii) synthetic pathway, key enzymes and naming;
A(iii) distribution and actions.
B. B(i) State the sites at which drugs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis.
C. C(i) Explain the basis of the therapeutic effects and side effects of blocking COX, and distinguish COX-1 from COX-2.

A. Describe the second enzymatic difficulty in the oxidation of an unsaturated fatty acid under the following headings:
A(i) when it arises;
A(ii) the double bond and the intermediate formed;
A(iii) why it is a problem.
B(i) Relate each enzyme of the correction to its reaction, cofactor and product.
C(i) Explain why the intermediate must be isomerised before β-oxidation continues, and why this leads to a lower yield.

A. Describe ATP synthase under the following headings:
A(i) location and structural components;
A(ii) mechanism of ATP synthesis (chemiosmotic coupling, binding-change mechanism, role of the c ring);
A(iii) stoichiometry (protons per ATP, ATP per rotation, P/O ratios).
B. B(i) Explain the basis of inhibition of ATP synthase by oligomycin and DCCD and how this differs from the action of uncouplers.
C. C(i) Distinguish between the F1 and F0 components.

A. Describe the mitochondrion under the following headings:
A(i) the membranes and their permeability;
A(ii) the compartments and the processes housed in each;
A(iii) origin and supporting evidence.
B. B(i) State how each part of the organelle relates to the inhibitors and uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation and their toxicity.
C. C(i) Explain how disruption of these parts leads to loss of ATP and other functional consequences, including useful uncoupling.

A. Describe the mitochondrial transport systems under the following headings:
A(i) the ATP-ADP translocase;
A(ii) the phosphate, dicarboxylate, tricarboxylate and pyruvate carriers;
A(iii) the shuttles that carry cytosolic NADH electrons into the mitochondrion.
B. B(i) Explain why shuttles are needed and the basis of inhibition of the translocase by atractyloside.
C. C(i) Distinguish between the glycerol 3-phosphate shuttle and the malate-aspartate shuttle, including ATP yield.

A. Describe the role of bile acids in lipid digestion under the following headings:
A(i) origin and chemical nature;
A(ii) micelle formation (critical micellar concentration, size, arrangement);
A(iii) mixed micelles and the transport of lipids across the unstirred layer, with the digestive enzymes involved.
B. B(i) Explain why absence of bile acids reduces lipid absorption and causes steatorrhoea, and which lipids depend most on bile acids.
C. C(i) Distinguish between micelles and emulsion droplets.

A. Describe peroxisomal and mitochondrial β-oxidation under the following headings:
A(i) substrates;
A(ii) entry;
A(iii) first oxidation;
A(iv) the hydration and dehydrogenation enzymes.
B(i) Explain why peroxisomal oxidation is less efficient and incomplete.
C(i) Distinguish between the two systems with respect to thiolase and the fate of the end products.

A. Describe the lipoproteins under the following headings:
A(i) general structure (core, surface) and size range;
A(ii) basis of classification and the five main classes with their source and role;
A(iii) composition and apolipoproteins of chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL, LDL and HDL.
B. Explain the clinical basis of the terms 'bad' and 'good' cholesterol:
B(i) why LDL is linked to atherosclerosis and HDL is protective;
B(ii) why plasma LDL rises when hepatocyte cholesterol is high.
C. Distinguish between LDL and HDL:
C(i) in function, composition and fate.

A. Describe the pancreas as an endocrine and exocrine organ under the following headings:
A(i) exocrine and endocrine parts;
A(ii) hormones of the islets of Langerhans and their actions;
A(iii) regulation of blood sugar.
B. B(i) State how the islet cell types relate to the common pathologies (types of diabetes and hyperinsulinism).
C. C(i) Explain why pancreatic disease produces effects in distant structures and functional deficits.

A. Describe the second and third reactions of the β-oxidation cycle under the following headings:
A(i) enzymes;
A(ii) substrates and stereochemistry;
A(iii) cofactors and products.
B(i) Explain why the hydratase step requires a particular double bond and how unsaturated fatty acids are affected.
C(i) Distinguish between the hydratase and the 3-L-hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase reactions, and between the cofactors of the first and third steps.

A. Describe the pathway of the steroid hormones under the following headings:
A(i) origin (starting material, activation, entry into the mitochondrion, first product);
A(ii) biosynthetic routes to aldosterone, cortisol and testosterone;
A(iii) transport and mechanism of action;
A(iv) metabolism of testosterone.
B. B(i) State where in the adrenal cortex each class is made.
C. C(i) Explain the biochemical basis of deficits or excesses of these hormones.

A. Describe the transfer of long-chain fatty acyl groups from the cytosol into the mitochondrial matrix under the following headings:
A(i) starting substrate and carrier compound;
A(ii) enzymes and their locations;
A(iii) the transport protein and the direction of movement of each molecule;
A(iv) the end result.
B(i) State the point at which the process is controlled.
B(ii) State the controlling metabolite.
C(i) Explain the origin of the controlling metabolite.
C(ii) Explain the condition under which the control operates.

A. Describe one cycle of mitochondrial β-oxidation of a saturated fatty acyl CoA under the following headings:
A(i) the first oxidation;
A(ii) hydration;
A(iii) the second oxidation;
A(iv) thiolytic cleavage and the products per round.
B(i) State how the reduced cofactor from the first oxidation is reoxidised and the ATP generated.
B(ii) State how the cycle is controlled by the redox state and by its own product.
C(i) State the fate of the acetyl CoA produced.

A. Give an overview of the β-oxidation of an unsaturated fatty acid such as linoleyl CoA under the following headings:
A(i) the initial rounds;
A(ii) the first problem;
A(iii) the second problem;
A(iv) resumption of the pathway.
B(i) Relate each problem to the enzyme step that is blocked and the enzyme that corrects it.
C(i) Explain why the effects appear as a deficit at a distant stage (the electron transport chain and ATP yield) compared with stearoyl CoA.

A. Describe the final reaction of the β-oxidation cycle under the following headings:
A(i) enzyme names;
A(ii) substrates and products;
A(iii) the total output of each round.
B(i) Explain how acetyl CoA is further oxidised and how ATP is obtained.
C(i) Distinguish between mitochondrial and peroxisomal thiolases, and between the control of thiolase and its product.

A. Describe the energy accounting for the complete oxidation of one palmitoyl-CoA under the following headings:
A(i) rounds and products;
A(ii) further oxidation of acetyl CoA;
A(iii) ATP from reduced cofactors;
A(iv) the net yield.
B(i) State the point at which reduced FAD enters the electron transport chain and the ATP per electron pair.
C(i) Explain the basis of the lower yield when a double bond bypasses acyl CoA dehydrogenase.

A. Describe the pathway of electrons in the respiratory chain under the following headings:
A(i) origin (sources and entry points);
A(ii) course through the complexes and carriers, with prosthetic groups;
A(iii) termination and protection against toxic oxygen products;
A(iv) proton pumping and the factors controlling the rate.
B. B(i) State the sites at which inhibitors block the chain.
C. C(i) Explain the effect of a block on the redox state of the carriers and on ATP formation.

A. Describe the development of the liver and biliary apparatus under the following headings:
A(i) Origin and growth;
A(ii) Tissue derivation;
A(iii) The biliary apparatus.
B(i) State how its parts relate to common anomalies.
C(i) Explain why a biliary defect causes jaundice and other functional consequences.

A. Describe the omenta and peritoneal ligaments under the following headings:
A(i) Definition;
A(ii) The lesser omentum;
A(iii) The greater omentum;
A(iv) Peritoneal ligaments of the liver, stomach and spleen.
B(i) Explain why the greater omentum is called the abdominal policeman.
C(i) Distinguish between the lesser and the greater omentum.

A. Describe the portal vein under the following headings:
A(i) Formation;
A(ii) Length, course and termination;
A(iii) Area drained;
A(iv) Direct and alternative routes.
B(i) State the surgical communications made to divert its blood.
B(ii) State the safe site for such a procedure.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the varices that develop in portal hypertension.

A. Describe the temporomandibular joint under the following headings:
A(i) Type and articular surfaces;
A(ii) Capsule, synovial membranes and compartments;
A(iii) Ligaments;
A(iv) Movements, innervation and blood supply.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of dislocation, ankylosis and other joint disorders.
C(i) Distinguish between the intrinsic and extrinsic ligaments.

A. Describe the nerve supply of the anterolateral abdominal wall under the following headings:
A(i) The thoracoabdominal and subcostal nerves (origin and muscles supplied);
A(ii) The contribution of the lumbar plexus;
A(iii) The segmental landmark at the umbilicus.
B(i) State the plane used for the transversus abdominis plane (TAP) block and what it achieves.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the muscle weakness that follows nerve injury and the pain from nerve entrapment.

A. Describe the rectus sheath under the following headings:
A(i) Definition and formation, with the lines of the anterior wall;
A(ii) Anterior and posterior walls;
A(iii) Contents;
A(iv) Functions.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of the pain arising at the lateral border of the rectus abdominis in chronic abdominal pain.
C(i) Distinguish between the rectus abdominis and the pyramidalis.

A. Describe the formation of the atrial septum under the following headings:
A(i) Septum primum and ostium primum;
A(ii) The AV septum and ostium secundum;
A(iii) Septum secundum and the oval foramen;
A(iv) Changes at birth.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of atrial septal defects and premature closure of the foramen ovale.
C(i) Distinguish between the septum primum and the septum secundum.

A. Describe the gross anatomy of the peritoneal cavity under the following headings:
A(i) The greater and lesser sacs;
A(ii) The supracolic and infracolic compartments;
A(iii) The paracolic gutters;
A(iv) The walls of the lesser sac and the omental foramen.
B(i) State how the folds and fossae of the anterolateral abdominal wall relate to hernias.
B(ii) State the clinical conditions related to the cavity.
C(i) Explain why infection may spread to a distant region and how the peritoneum is innervated.

A. Describe the development of the urinary bladder and urethra under the following headings:
A(i) The urogenital sinus and its parts;
A(ii) The bladder;
A(iii) The urachus;
A(iv) The urethra.
B(i) State how its parts relate to congenital anomalies.
C(i) Explain how a defect of the cloaca may involve distant structures.

A. Describe the development of the stomach under the following headings:
A(i) Growth;
A(ii) Histological differentiation;
A(iii) Position adjustment;
A(iv) Mesenteries and the omental bursa.
B(i) Explain the embryologic basis of pyloric stenosis in infants.
C(i) Distinguish between the dorsal and ventral mesogastrium and their derivatives.

A. Describe the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct under the following headings:
A(i) Origin;
A(ii) Course and termination;
A(iii) Branches and derivatives in each sex.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on the duct.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of duplication of the ureter and the ectopic opening.

A. Describe the nerve, blood and lymphatic supply of the tongue under the following headings:
A(i) Motor supply;
A(ii) Sensory supply of the anterior two-thirds and posterior one-third;
A(iii) Arterial supply and venous drainage;
A(iv) Lymphatic drainage.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on the tongue.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of functional loss and the related clinical conditions.

A. Describe the gross anatomy of the lumbar vertebrae under the following headings:
A(i) Number and general features;
A(ii) A typical lumbar vertebra;
A(iii) The fifth lumbar vertebra;
A(iv) Joints and ligaments.
B(i) State how their development relates to spina bifida and hemivertebra.
B(ii) State the pathologies of the foramen and the curvature.
C(i) Explain how a narrow canal causes a functional deficit, stating the canal contents.

A. Describe the derivation and innervation of the larynx under the following headings:
A(i) Embryonic origin of lining, muscle and cartilage;
A(ii) The nerve and its branches;
A(iii) Derivatives of the 4th and 6th arches;
A(iv) Development of the laryngeal cavity.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on these nerves.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the deficit after nerve injury.

A. Describe the development of the eye under the following headings:
A(i) Germ layer origins;
A(ii) The optic cup and lens;
A(iii) Retina, iris and ciliary body;
A(iv) Other structures.
B(i) State how its parts relate to congenital anomalies.
C(i) Explain why a maternal infection may cause distant defects together with eye defects.

A. Describe the inferior epigastric artery under the following headings:
A(i) Origin;
A(ii) Course and relations;
A(iii) Branches and related peritoneal folds;
A(iv) Anastomoses and venous drainage.
B(i) State its relation to the inguinal rings and the hernias.
B(ii) State the safe site for a procedure relative to it.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the bleeding after injury.

A. Describe the path of blood in the fetal circulation under the following headings:
A(i) Origin of the oxygenated blood;
A(ii) Course through the shunts;
A(iii) The pulmonary route.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on these vessels.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the changes at birth and the remnants formed.

A. Describe the arterial supply of the embryonic gut under the following headings:
A(i) Origin;
A(ii) The vessels formed for each segment;
A(iii) Branches and distribution;
A(iv) Boundaries of the segments.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on these vessels.
C(i) Explain the embryologic basis of the injury after their occlusion.

A. Describe the inguinal canal under the following headings:
A(i) Situation, shape and dimensions;
A(ii) Openings;
A(iii) Walls;
A(iv) Contents.
B(i) Explain why it is a common site of herniation and which parts of its walls are reinforced.
B(ii) State the function of the canal in both sexes.
C(i) Distinguish between the deep and the superficial inguinal rings.

A. Describe the development of the skin under the following headings:
A(i) Epidermis;
A(ii) Dermis;
A(iii) Glands, hair and nails.
B(i) State how its parts relate to common pathologies.
C(i) Explain how defects of migrating cells and signalling cause distant or patchy functional deficits.

A. Describe the peritoneum under the following headings:
A(i) Definition and layers;
A(ii) The peritoneal cavity and fluid;
A(iii) Functions;
A(iv) Relationship of viscera to peritoneum.
B(i) Explain why the female peritoneal cavity is a potential pathway of infection and the use of the peritoneum in dialysis.
C(i) Distinguish between the parietal and visceral peritoneum.

A. Describe the superior mesenteric artery under the following headings:
A(i) Origin;
A(ii) Course and relations;
A(iii) Branches;
A(iv) Distribution.
B(i) State the safe site for a procedure on it.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the deformity after its injury.

A. Describe the development of the permanent kidney under the following headings:
A(i) The three sets of kidneys;
A(ii) The two origins of the metanephros;
A(iii) The collecting system;
A(iv) The nephron and the position of the kidney.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of renal agenesis and horseshoe kidney.
C(i) Distinguish between the ureteric bud and the metanephric blastema.

A. Describe the development of the lung under the following headings:
A(i) Origin and derivation of tissues;
A(ii) Branching of the bronchial tree;
A(iii) Periods of maturation.
B(i) State how its parts relate to congenital anomalies.
C(i) Explain the functional basis of the air-blood barrier and lung growth after birth.

A. Describe the bony pelvis under the following headings:
A(i) The sacrum;
A(ii) The hip bone;
A(iii) The pelvic girdle and the true and false pelves.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of the features of the female pelvis and the obstetric landmark.
C(i) Distinguish between the male and female pelvis.

A. Describe the lumbar plexus under the following headings:
A(i) Formation and location;
A(ii) Branches and root values;
A(iii) Relations of the branches to psoas major;
A(iv) Distribution.
B(i) State the site at which the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is entrapped.
B(ii) State the safe site for a procedure on the plexus.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the symptom after entrapment and the muscles supplied.

A. Describe the development of the ear under the following headings:
A(i) Its three parts and their origins;
A(ii) The internal ear;
A(iii) The middle ear;
A(iv) The external ear.
B(i) Explain the clinical basis of preauricular sinus and other ear malformations.
C(i) Distinguish between the ossicles from the first and second arches, and between pharyngeal groove and pouch.

A. Describe the lumbar plexus under the following headings:
A(i) formation and location;
A(ii) branches and root values;
A(iii) relations of the branches to psoas major;
A(iv) distribution.
B(i) State the site at which the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is entrapped.
C(i) Explain the anatomical basis of the symptom after entrapment and the muscles supplied.

